// The walk on spheres over WoSX's default tree with a point's walks
// deferred step by step onto a queue of the point's own, the queue drained
// in gangs of sixteen lanes: the CPU form of fcpwb-gpu-defer-block.bonsai,
// and the packet schedules' gang (fcpwb-packet-16-threads.bonsai) with the
// walks compacted. In the packet schedule a gang is sixteen walks of one
// point run from their first step to their last, so the gang runs as many
// passes as its longest walk has steps and a lane whose walk ended idles
// through the rest (a walk is 13.7 steps on the head by mean, the longest
// of sixteen far more). Here `solve_all.defer(walk, steps)` pushes every
// walk's first step onto the point's queue and `walk.defer(walk, steps)`
// pushes each step's successor, so the drain's round r runs the r-th step
// of every walk of the point still going and nothing else: a gang holds
// sixteen live steps while the point has sixteen walks live, and the
// rounds thin only as the walks end. The traversal is the packet one --
// the gang vectorized before the closest-point query is sorted and put on
// its stack, one node for the gang and each child entered by the lanes
// whose query reaches it, FCPW's CPU order by the majority of the lanes --
// so what changes against fcpwb-packet-16-threads is the lanes a gang
// holds, not what a gang does with them. The queue is the point's
// (`queue(i)`), its storage the thread's arena since the point loop is
// bound to the threads; the pass over the record after the rounds adds
// each walk's value into the point's sums. The cost against the packet is
// a queue entry per step -- the position, distance, step count and
// generator written and read once per step -- and the rounds' loop; a
// gang's lanes are walks of one point at one step count, as the packet's
// are, so the leaves a gang reaches are as spread as the packet's.
import apps/rtq/schedules/layouts/fcpwb;

schedule {
    steps = solve_all.queue(i);
    solve_all.defer(walk, steps);
    walk.defer(walk, steps);
    solve_all.split(steps, steps_gang, steps_lane, 16, true)
             .vectorize(steps_lane);
    closest_within.sort(triangles.Interior,
                        |i : u32, s : Sphere, lo : vector[vec3f, 2], hi : vector[vec3f, 2]|
                        select(distmin(s, AABB{lo[0u], hi[0u]}) == 0.0 &&
                               distmin(s, AABB{lo[1u], hi[1u]}) == 0.0,
                               distmax(s, AABB{lo[i], hi[i]}),
                               distmin(s, AABB{lo[i], hi[i]})))
                  .loopify(64);
    closest_within.skip(closest_point_fcpw);
    solve_all.bind(i, CPUThread);
}
